{"packet_id":"D025-01","doc_id":"D025","jurisdiction":"CA","category":"security_deposits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"California security deposit cap and return deadline","requirement":"A landlord may not demand or receive security above one month’s rent plus the first month’s rent, and must return the remainder with an itemized statement within 21 calendar days after the tenant vacates.","key_value":"1 month’s rent in addition to the first month’s rent","coverage_conditions":"Applies to security for a rental agreement for residential property used as the tenant’s dwelling. Subdivision (c) does not apply to security collected or demanded before July 1, 2024.","applicability":{"conditions":[],"per_tenancy":"Subdivision (c) does not apply to a security collected or demanded by the landlord before July 1, 2024.","coverage_quotes":[]},"exemptions":"Advance payment of six months’ rent or more if the lease is six months or longer; natural-person landlords (or LLCs of natural persons) owning no more than two residential rental properties with no more than four dwelling units may take up to two months’ rent.","penalty":"A landlord that in bad faith fails to comply with the itemized-statement and return requirements is not entitled to claim any amount of the security.","effective_date":"2024-07-01","valid_through":null,"citation":"Cal. Civ. Code § 1950.5","quoted_span":"Except as provided in paragraph (2), (3), or (5), a landlord shall not demand or receive security, however denominated, in an amount or value in excess of an amount equal to one month’s rent, in addition to any rent for the first month paid on or before initial occupancy.","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.95,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D025-01","doc_id":"D025","jurisdiction":"CA","category":"screening_restrictions","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Service member security and rental application protections","requirement":"A landlord may not refuse to rent to a service member because the small-landlord deposit limit bars charging more security, and must give a written explanation when charging a service member higher security based on credit history, returning the excess after six months.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Service members, as defined in Section 400 of the Military and Veterans Code, who rent residential property in which the service member will reside.","applicability":{"conditions":[],"per_tenancy":"Applies to a prospective tenant who is a service member, as defined in Section 400 of the Military and Veterans Code.","coverage_quotes":[]},"exemptions":"The two-month security limit for qualifying small landlords does not apply if the prospective tenant is a service member.","penalty":null,"effective_date":"2025-04-01","valid_through":null,"citation":"Cal. Civ. Code § 1950.5","quoted_span":"On or after April 1, 2025, if a landlord or its agent charges a service member who rents residential property in which the service member will reside a higher than standard or advertised security pursuant to paragraph (1) due to the credit history, credit score, housing history, or other factor related to the tenant, the landlord shall provide the tenant with a written statement, on or before the date the lease is signed, of the amount of the higher security and an explanation why the higher security amount is being charged.","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.9,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
